# What never enters the build context. Docker does NOT read `.gitignore`, so
# every artefact `server/.gitignore` keeps out of the repo has to be named
# again here or it rides into the image from the developer's own working tree.
#
# Two of these are correctness, not size, and they are the reason this file
# exists at all (web-and-households ticket 09):
#
#   static/app    The Vite bundle. Stage 1 of the Dockerfile builds it fresh;
#                 `COPY . .` in stage 2 would otherwise lay the DEVELOPER's
#                 last local build down first. `COPY` merges into a directory
#                 rather than replacing it, so files from an older build with
#                 different content hashes would survive alongside the new
#                 ones, and `collectstatic` would hash and ship them. The
#                 image would serve a mix of two builds with nothing broken
#                 enough to notice.
#   staticfiles/  `collectstatic`'s output, including `staticfiles.json` - the
#                 manifest `CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage` resolves
#                 every `{% static %}` name through. Same shape: the build
#                 rewrites the manifest but not the stale hashed files beside
#                 it.
#
# Everything else here is size or hygiene.
.venv/
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/.tanstack/
frontend/dist/
static/app/
staticfiles/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# A secret must never be baked into an image layer. `.env` lives in `deploy/`
# and is outside this context anyway; this line is the guard for the day
# somebody drops one in here instead.
.env

# pytest's own output from a local run. `junit-after.xml` was sitting in this
# directory when the image was first written.
junit*.xml

# `server/tests/` is deliberately NOT excluded. `requirements.txt` already
# installs pytest and pytest-django into the image, the suite is about 1 MB of
# source, and being able to run it inside the running container against the
# real Postgres is a diagnostic worth having on migration night (ticket 13,
# step 3) - "does the engine agree with its own database on this box" is not a
# question a laptop can answer. Excluding them would save a megabyte and cost
# that.
